            content: "<p>Portugal's meteorological agency issued an extreme heatwave warning in early July 2026, with temperatures in several inland regions of the country expected to peak at 43 degrees Celsius. This heatwave marks one of the earliest and most intense in recent years, triggering serious concerns over wildfire risk and health impacts for residents, particularly vulnerable groups like the elderly and children.</p><p>Inland regions like Alentejo and parts of Beira Interior are expected to be the most affected areas, with temperatures far exceeding historical averages for the same period. Local authorities have activated heat emergency protocols, including the provision of public cooling centers and restrictions on outdoor activities during the hottest hours.</p><p>Wildfire risk is a major government concern given drying vegetation conditions from a combination of extreme temperatures and minimal rainfall throughout spring 2026. The Instituto Português do Mar e da Atmosfera (IPMA) has raised fire alert status in several districts, urging residents to avoid activities that could trigger sparks in fire-prone areas.</p><p>Climate experts link Portugal's increasingly intense and frequent heatwaves to long-term climate change trends affecting the Mediterranean region as a whole, warning that Portugal's future summers could face similarly extreme or worse conditions if global warming trends are not addressed collectively.</p>",
